    <h1>Social Selection of Leaders as a Mechanism of the Historical Competitiveness of Societies</h1>
    
    <div class="meta">
        <strong>Author:</strong> Andriy Nosalsky<br>
        <strong>Document Version:</strong> v1.1 (Updated: September 9, 2026)<br>
        <strong>Status:</strong> Private authorial record / Open Access Academic Framework
    </div>

    <div class="epigraph">
        "Talent needs help, mediocrity will break through on its own."<br>
        — Lev Adolfovich Goldberg, 1961
    </div>

    <h2>1. Central Hypothesis</h2>
    <p>The historical productivity and competitiveness of a society are determined to a significant extent not by its territory, natural resources, or even individual cultural characteristics, but by the effectiveness of the mechanism through which society determines who has the right to become a leader and enter the elite.</p>
    
    <div class="formula">
        social subconscious &rarr; image of the legitimate leader &rarr; social selection mechanism &rarr; composition of the elite &rarr; institutions &rarr; societal productivity &rarr; historical competitiveness.
    </div>
    
    <p>The question “Who governs society?” is derived from a deeper question: “What type of person does this society consider worthy of being at the top?”</p>

    <h2>2. The Social Subconscious and the Image of the Leader</h2>
    <p>The starting point of the concept is the assumption that people do not simply rationally evaluate candidates for power. Society contains a certain collective, largely unconscious image of what a “real” leader should be. This image may include strength, wealth, ancestry, courage, cunning, competence, morality, the ability to protect, create, win, and lead others.</p>
    <p>A theoretical bridge to this proposition is Carl Jung’s concept of archetypes and the collective unconscious. At the same time, Nosalskyi’s concept is not reducible to Jungian psychology: Jung can be regarded here as one way of describing the mechanism by which an image of a socially legitimate leader is formed.</p>

    <h2>3. The Drive for Social Dominance and the Problem of Selection</h2>
    <p>If people seek to increase their social status, every society inevitably has to answer the question: who exactly is granted the right to be at the top? Different societies give different answers: aristocratic (ancestry), oligarchic (wealth), clientelist (loyalty and connections), coercive (force), conformist (conformity to group norms), or meritocratic (competence, results, creation of something new, and contribution to society). In this model, the elite is viewed as the product of a particular mechanism of social selection.</p>

    <h2>4. From a Model of Dominance to a Model of Creation</h2>
    <p>The most primitive form is: “I am stronger than you.” A potentially higher level is: “I am so strong that I can lift you up with me.” In such a system, the highest social status is granted not to the person who merely controls others, but to the person who increases the capabilities of other people. Leadership ceases to be merely control over resources and people and becomes the ability to create new resources, opportunities, capabilities, and other strong people.</p>

    <h2>5. Picaro and Janteloven as Two Poles of Social Behavior</h2>
    <p>Picaro symbolizes individual initiative, risk, ingenuity, action beyond established boundaries, outsider energy, and the desire to rise through one’s own efforts. Janteloven symbolizes the pressure of conformity, distrust of excessive individual success, social leveling, and group control over individual behavior. A productive society must create a narrow corridor between them: allow a strong individual to rise, while directing that strength toward creating new opportunities for others.</p>

    <h2>6. Historical Evolution of Social Constructions</h2>
    <p>The author’s model proposes the following cycle: environment &rarr; need &rarr; social construction &rarr; elite &rarr; meme &rarr; mass behavior &rarr; result &rarr; change in the environment &rarr; crisis of the old construction &rarr; generational change &rarr; new meme &rarr; new social construction. Social systems do not arise in a vacuum. Changes in the external environment create new problems; an old method of selecting leaders may cease to fit new conditions. Through generational change, the spread of new memes, and institutional changes, a new construction emerges. History can be viewed as an evolution of ways of organizing social selection.</p>

    <h2>7. Westphalian Competition as a Selection Environment</h2>
    <p>If states constantly compete with one another, an external criterion of effectiveness emerges: a society that organizes resources, innovation, governance, and the mobilization of people more effectively gains a competitive advantage. Interstate competition can act as a kind of evolutionary filter for social constructions.</p>
    <p>Hypothesis: systems that are better at selecting competent, productive leaders capable of creating something new have a long-term advantage over systems where access to power is determined primarily by ancestry, loyalty, or the capture of resources.</p>

    <h2>8. Ethics of Supporting the Progressor</h2>
    <p>Original authorial formulation: “Someone is doing something — help. Someone has an idea — support it, do not steal it. Otherwise there will be no progress, and the people dear to you will suffer. Respect and support progressors.” Condensed principle: “See progress — support the progressor.” Its essence is not limited to the protection of intellectual property. Appropriating ideas or destroying initiative creates a negative incentive for future creators. In the long term, society loses not only a particular idea, but also future ideas that might have emerged in an environment safe for creativity. Therefore, supporting a progressor can be viewed as a social investment.</p>

    <h2>9. A Possible New Ethical Model</h2>
    <p>Historical turning points changed not only institutions, but also people’s ideas about what is right, permissible, and prestigious. The author’s hypothesis is that contemporary society may require the next ethical transformation. Its core may be not merely “do not interfere with another person,” but: “if a person creates something that increases society’s capabilities, support that person.” This means a transition from competition for status to competition in creating opportunities; from dominance over people to increasing people’s capabilities.</p>

    <h2>10. Summary Formula of the Concept</h2>
    <div class="formula">
        A person seeks social status.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        Society forms an image of who deserves high status.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        This image determines the mechanism of social selection.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        The selection mechanism forms the composition of the elite.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        The elite forms or changes institutions.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        Institutions determine incentives for mass behavior.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        Mass behavior determines societal productivity.<br>
        &darr;<br>
        Productivity determines its ability to compete in the historical environment.
    </div>
    <p>Main thesis: “The historical competitiveness of a society is significantly determined by how it transforms people’s drive for social dominance into a mechanism for selecting competent, creative, responsible leaders capable of increasing the capabilities of others.”</p>

    <h2>11. The Demilitarization of Progress: Overcoming the "Chicken-or-Egg" Dilemma of Historical Evolution</h2>
    <p>Historically, human societies have been trapped in a destructive evolutionary cycle where war and existential geopolitical conflicts served as the primary drivers of scientific and technological progress. Under the classical Westphalian system, the mechanism of selection was external and coercive: states were forced to innovate, mobilize resources, and advance knowledge simply to avoid physical annihilation or subjugation. In this paradigm, innovation was merely a byproduct of the military-industrial complex.</p>
    <p>The core objective of the proposed concept is to break this violent cycle and fundamentally alter the historical sequence. By shifting the causal primacy to the internal mechanism of leader selection, society gains the capacity to decouple progress from warfare.</p>
